scientists are at war with kovat 19  dozens of laboratories are now  battlegrounds in the race for a vaccine  here at Oxford University  volunteers blood samples are prepared  for testing ahead of the first human  trials of a vaccine the blood will be  checked for antibodies and any t-cell  response to the virus t-cells can  recognize and destroy infected cells  and here in Germany one small company  part funded by Microsoft billionaire  Bill Gates says it too will soon have a  vaccine ready for human trials it may  look like slow work but things are  moving at breakneck speed it can take  decades to develop a vaccine this lab  wants one not sooner people are saying  to 18 months which of course is much  much shorter than a normal development  for vaccines that can be 10 15 even 20  years this is the enemy  Korona means crown in Latin named  because of the spikes on the virus's  outer coat this crown is also its  weakness the vaccines under development  contain the genetic sequence of this  surface spike which Prime's the immune  system to attack the virus if it later  infects the body but what about those  most at risk of contracting the virus  symptoms can range from a runny nose to  a sore throat to a fever but it can  escalate quickly among people with  respiratory problems or underlying  health issues into full-blown pneumonia  one team of British scientist believes  our best line of defense lies deep in  our lungs they've developed this inhaler  to help restore levels of a substance  called interferon beta in people's lungs  vital for removing viruses from the body  unhealthy lungs lack interferon beta  levels also naturally deplete with age  which is why elderly people are more a  risk of contracting the virus patients  with lung disease diabetes pre-existing  heart and cardiovascular disease they  get the inhalation once a day for two  weeks to increase the viral immunity in  the lung to prevent the virus from  replicating and eliminating the virus  more effectively the virus has led to an  acceleration in engineering this rapid  kovat 19 test has been developed by the  German company Bosch blood samples are  inserted into the cartridge for analysis  it takes two and a half hours for a red  zone  and it's 95 percent accurate but it  could be many months before the  equipment's readily available meanwhile  a number of firms including the British  vacuum maker Dyson aeroplane maker  Airbus on the Formula One team McLaren  are now racing to develop breathing aids  to meet urgent demand no single company  or laboratory can do this alone  it requires international effort and  resources the world's best brains have  been mobilized our greatest hope lies in  science Nev Barker al-jazeera London
 

